WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:20.520
Tim Ferriss: So sleep and sex. Let's dive&nbsp;
into this wide and exciting topic. Where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:27.180
should we begin in a discussion of sleep and sex?

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:33.060
Dr. Matthew Walker: Maybe we could&nbsp;
start with how sleep impacts sex, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:38.280
begin with actually the literal&nbsp;
idea of separating those bedfellows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:43.967
and to the question of, should you have a sleep&nbsp;
divorce as a couple to prevent a real one?

00:00:43.967 --> 00:00:46.240
Tim Ferriss: And sleep divorce&nbsp;
is sleeping separately?

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:51.360
Dr. Matthew Walker: That's right. Yeah. So a&nbsp;
sleep divorce is where you will sleep either&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:57.420
in separate locations or at least in separate&nbsp;
beds. The diluted version of the sleep divorce,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:01.440
rather than sleeping in separate rooms and&nbsp;
separate beds is called the Scandinavian method,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:05.940
which you think — it sounds so much&nbsp;
more salacious than it actually is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:09.780
which is, essentially, you've just got two&nbsp;
separate beds that you put side by side,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:15.480
and that's the diet version of a sleep&nbsp;
divorce. But I do think that sleep divorce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:19.980
idea is important because it is a taboo, and&nbsp;
it also can markedly improve things for people.

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:28.020
So when we've looked at survey data, both in&nbsp;
the US and also in the UK with The Sleep Council&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:34.020
there, what you find is that about one out of&nbsp;
every four couples who are surveyed will tell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:38.580
you that they have had a sleep divorce,&nbsp;
that they will sleep in separate beds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:44.940
And we think that that may be in part&nbsp;
an underestimate because of the stigma&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:49.920
associated with it. And if you survey people&nbsp;
anonymously, you get to a number of — almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:55.620
one out of every three people will admit to&nbsp;
waking up at least in different locations.

00:01:56.760 --> 00:02:02.460
And there is pretty good evidence from the&nbsp;
science as to why that number may make sense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:08.460
why 25 percent of all couples suggest&nbsp;
that — when we study couples who are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:14.100
sleeping together objectively on almost every&nbsp;
measure that we can quantify about sleep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:19.740
sleep is worse when you are sleeping as a couple&nbsp;
relative to when you are sleeping separately.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:26.280
The twist in the tail there though is that when&nbsp;
you ask people about the satisfaction of sleep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:31.380
there is definitely some group of individuals&nbsp;
that say, "Look, I feel more satisfied with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:35.820
my sleep when I'm sleeping with my partner&nbsp;
than when I'm sleeping separately," despite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:42.240
the fact that objectively their sleep is worse.&nbsp;
So I'm not suggesting it's a one size fits all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:49.020
but I think the taboo comes from the idea&nbsp;
that, "Well, if you're not sleeping together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:54.141
then you are not sleeping together."&nbsp;
And the exact opposite is true.

00:02:54.141 --> 00:02:55.240
Tim Ferriss: That's British for having&nbsp;
intercourse? Is that what that means?

00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:59.700
Dr. Matthew Walker: Thank you very much, yeah.&nbsp;
We just get over this. It's my desperately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:05.280
unfortunate Hugh Grant genes that has me&nbsp;
trying to navigate around the topic rather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:09.420
than just saying it straight. So thank you,&nbsp;
Tim. Yes. If you're not sleeping together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:12.467
then you're not having sex,&nbsp;
you're not having intercourse.

00:03:12.467 --> 00:03:13.060
Tim Ferriss: Yes.

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:19.440
Dr. Matthew Walker: But the — oh, come&nbsp;
on, Matthew. Hold it together. But it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:25.080
turns out that the opposite is true, that when&nbsp;
you get a couple who are sleeping well, their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:31.020
sex life actually improves. And it's probably for&nbsp;
three reasons that we've uncovered. The first is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:40.620
hormones, the second is sensitivity, and the third&nbsp;
is libido. And I'll try to park my Hugh Grant and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:44.460
get right into it. In terms of the hormones —
Tim Ferriss: Take off the gloves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:46.140
Matt, take off the gloves.
Dr. Matthew Walker: Come on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:51.780
It's time. I'm wondering what you mean by saying&nbsp;
take off the glove in terms of this conversation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:56.611
but let me just skip it. You see, you opened&nbsp;
the door, Tim, and I walked right into it.&nbsp;

00:03:56.611 --> 00:03:58.120
Tim Ferriss: Oh, here we go. Here we&nbsp;
go. Or put on the gloves, if you prefer.&nbsp;

00:03:58.120 --> 00:04:02.340
Dr. Matthew Walker: Yeah, I think probably&nbsp;
best — but we know each other quite well now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:08.820
so maybe — anyway, so in terms of the hormones.&nbsp;
Firstly, we know that testosterone, gosh, it takes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:15.120
a pretty sharp nose dive in males, and in females&nbsp;
when you're not sleeping well. Males who, if we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:21.480
put them on a diet of maybe four or five hours&nbsp;
of sleep, they drop their levels of testosterone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:28.560
somewhere by about 10 years of aging.&nbsp;
So a lack of sleep will age a man by a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:34.440
decade in terms of virility. It's also true&nbsp;
of estrogen in women. And so when those two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:39.720
sex hormones are not in play, you get a&nbsp;
reduction in the quality of the sex life.&nbsp;

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:43.800
The second component, and this is data&nbsp;
that we've only really got in females,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:53.460
less so in males. But the sensitivity of female&nbsp;
genitalia increases when sleep is in high volume&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:58.740
versus when people are not getting sufficient&nbsp;
sleep. And we think that's due to the estrogen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.740 --> 00:05:04.140
that when estrogen is in normative amounts because&nbsp;
you're sleeping well, there is greater vaginal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:10.080
lubrication, which therefore leads to greater&nbsp;
sensitivity and greater pleasure by way of sex.&nbsp;

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:18.900
And then the final aspect is libido. We've&nbsp;
also found that when a woman obtains an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:27.000
extra one hour of sleep, there is a 14 percent&nbsp;
increase in her desirability to be intimate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:33.420
to have sex with her partner. And I find that&nbsp;
interesting because if you put it in context,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:39.720
the FDA-approved drugs for increased libido in&nbsp;
women, things like, I think it's called Vyleesi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:44.280
it's one of them I know. That will&nbsp;
increase libido in women by about 24&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:48.900
percent. But here is the simple addition&nbsp;
of one hour of extra sleep, you can get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.900 --> 00:05:54.480
more than 50 percent of that benefit drug free.
Tim Ferriss: Question for you, Matt. How are these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.480 --> 00:06:01.800
drug companies measuring increases in libido? Is&nbsp;
it a self-reported one-to-10 scale or something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:06.720
like that, which can be very sort of fungible?
Dr. Matthew Walker: It is self-reported. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:12.900
many of those scales are subject. It's very&nbsp;
much like pain. When it comes to libido, it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:17.700
somewhat difficult to quantify. And of course,&nbsp;
it's not just about innate biological libido,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.700 --> 00:06:20.880
the conditions have to be right. You have&nbsp;
to have the relationship with your partner.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.420 --> 00:06:24.900
Which actually also reminds me, by the way,&nbsp;
when couples are not sleeping well — and this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:30.900
is a study from UC Berkeley, not from my lab. But&nbsp;
what they found is that they have more fights,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.900 --> 00:06:35.760
they don't resolve conflict nearly as well&nbsp;
between them when they're having a fight. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:40.560
the reason is because you lose empathy. When&nbsp;
you are not sleeping well, your ability to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:45.540
empathize with other individuals, and we now have&nbsp;
demonstrated why in terms of the brain networks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.540 --> 00:06:51.900
You decrease that capacity for understanding&nbsp;
the other. And no wonder you're fighting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:55.260
and you're fighting in a pretty poor way.
Tim Ferriss: What's the playbook?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.260 --> 00:07:00.840
What's the best practices?
Dr. Matthew Walker: I'm not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.840 --> 00:07:05.820
suggesting that a sleep divorce is for everyone&nbsp;
by any means whatsoever. There are some people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.820 --> 00:07:10.800
for whom they adore sleeping with their partner&nbsp;
for lots of reasons, safety, security, intimacy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:18.240
But I think if you are interested in it, take&nbsp;
a graded approach. And I would suggest firstly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:24.240
just having an open, gentle conversation, and&nbsp;
don't be defensive about it. The second is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.040 --> 00:07:31.200
don't suggest that it's permanent. Offer&nbsp;
the idea that, "Look, could we do this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:35.040
darling? Could we do this for the next&nbsp;
week or the next two weeks? And I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:38.580
not suggesting it's forever. Let's just&nbsp;
see, and let's just try it on for size."&nbsp;

00:07:39.900 --> 00:07:47.880
Then I think what people misunderstand about&nbsp;
sleeping together in the same bed is what they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.880 --> 00:07:52.800
miss. They don't really miss the majority of time,&nbsp;
because for the majority of time you're asleep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:57.540
you're unconscious. What you really miss are the&nbsp;
bookends of sleep. That sort of getting into bed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.540 --> 00:08:03.180
having a cuddle, saying "good night," and in the&nbsp;
morning, waking up and doing the same thing. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.180 --> 00:08:09.240
if you have a sleep divorce, what you can also do&nbsp;
is build that in. So whoever goes to bed first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.240 --> 00:08:13.980
the other person comes in, you have your time,&nbsp;
you sort of cuddle, do whatever you need to do.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.980 --> 00:08:19.020
And then you leave and repeat the same process in&nbsp;
the morning. You don't have to do that every day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.020 --> 00:08:24.420
of course, it's not going to be practical. But&nbsp;
in that way, you sort of get the benefits of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.420 --> 00:08:30.000
sleeping together whilst still having a&nbsp;
sleep divorce, if that makes some sense.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:34.860
So I would just say be honest with yourself.
Tim Ferriss: I feel like sleep divorce needs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.860 --> 00:08:39.780
a rebrand. Maybe furniture polyamory, since&nbsp;
you'd be on separate pieces of furniture.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.780 --> 00:08:47.220
Sounds really sort of risqué, taboo, kind of sexy.
Dr. Matthew Walker: I am so stealing that because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.220 --> 00:08:51.120
when you bring that up in a dinner conversation&nbsp;
and you finally admit it to your friends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:59.100
they all think, "Oh, no, they're on the rocks."&nbsp;
But furniture polyamory, all sorts of swings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.100 --> 00:09:00.720
from the ceiling and —
Tim Ferriss: Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:03.900
it sounds very exciting. Very exciting.
Dr. Matthew Walker: Yeah. Yeah. Candle wax&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.900 --> 00:09:05.220
on the nipples, let's go.
Tim Ferriss: They're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.220 --> 00:09:09.060
killing it, yeah.
Dr. Matthew Walker: Exactly. "Honey, we've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.060 --> 00:09:13.140
got to switch things up if they're doing that."
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. So a question that has always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.580 --> 00:09:20.280
been unanswered for me. I have some theories, but&nbsp;
why is it — and this is my assumption just based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:24.960
on broad strokes, having had conversations&nbsp;
with many people about this and observed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:31.560
this also online. It seems that men tend&nbsp;
to run hot and women tend to run cooler,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.560 --> 00:09:37.560
in the sense that men will often want the&nbsp;
temperature in the room to be lower, the sheets or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.560 --> 00:09:42.660
the bedding to be thinner. I mean, this is part of&nbsp;
the reason why I use devices like Eight Sleep's,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.660 --> 00:09:45.660
not the only thing out there. They do sponsor&nbsp;
the podcast, but I used them before that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.660 --> 00:09:53.700
so that you can have zones of temperature. I don't&nbsp;
know if this is something you've looked into it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.700 --> 00:10:00.660
is that mostly attributable to muscle mass&nbsp;
and thermogenesis? Is it hormonal? How do you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.660 --> 00:10:04.080
explain that if — do you have an explanation?
Dr. Matthew Walker: Yeah, it's muscle mass,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:09.720
thermogenesis. It's also regarding — and&nbsp;
thermogenesis really comes onto metabolic rate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.720 --> 00:10:16.380
and then it's also hormonal based on testosterone,&nbsp;
which is in part a thermogenic hormone in and of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.380 --> 00:10:21.660
itself. That temperature difference, we&nbsp;
can measure and we can see it. Actually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.660 --> 00:10:27.060
studies were done before we were looking at&nbsp;
this, not related to sleep, but just related to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.060 --> 00:10:33.540
core body temperature. And they demonstrated that&nbsp;
there are some age dependencies to it though, that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.540 --> 00:10:40.140
once you get past about 50 or 60, the differences&nbsp;
between men and women start to collapse and they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.140 --> 00:10:45.960
start to become much more similar to each other&nbsp;
than they are different. Why is that? It could be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.960 --> 00:10:50.400
in part because of the reduction in testosterone.&nbsp;
That happens also because of the muscle mass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.400 --> 00:10:56.700
degradation that occurs in males as well. But&nbsp;
that evidence of males in general, running hot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.700 --> 00:11:03.660
is well demonstrated, and it does bleed over into&nbsp;
