00:11:44.760 --> 00:11:48.480
could we just venture into the bedroom? Of&nbsp;
course. But it's because I've had really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:52.127
rough sleep the past couple of nights.&nbsp;
Can you just like — please knock me out?"&nbsp;

00:11:52.127 --> 00:11:54.960
Tim Ferriss: That's a crowd pleaser.
Dr. Matthew Walker: Yeah. Isn't it just — there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.960 --> 00:11:59.880
a real divorce rather than a sleep one. But&nbsp;
I think the evidence is actually favorable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.880 --> 00:12:05.880
that when we survey couples, what we find is&nbsp;
that sleep — although there's a caveat here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.720 --> 00:12:14.040
Sex, as long as it's associated with orgasm —&nbsp;
and I'm not pointing to any one of the two sexes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.040 --> 00:12:20.280
maybe males. But please keep this in mind&nbsp;
that sex, as long as it accomplishes orgasm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.280 --> 00:12:28.080
is associated with about 70 percent increase in&nbsp;
reported sleep quality that following night, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.080 --> 00:12:35.460
people do fall asleep faster as a consequence. And&nbsp;
we're learning about the mechanisms, but I would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.460 --> 00:12:40.020
say that it's probably quite depressing to hear&nbsp;
that news if you are, let's say you are single,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.020 --> 00:12:45.960
or you don't have a partner, or your partner is&nbsp;
away for a while. You don't have to worry too much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.560 --> 00:12:52.140
because it turns out that masturbation is&nbsp;
a reasonable stand in. And what we've also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.140 --> 00:12:56.520
discovered is that masturbation, again,&nbsp;
as long as it's associated with orgasm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.520 --> 00:13:03.780
produces a 47 percent increase in your sleep&nbsp;
quality. And actually, it will decrease the speed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.780 --> 00:13:09.300
with which you fall asleep by about 50 percent.&nbsp;
And you'll hear this a lot, some people will say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.300 --> 00:13:14.340
they use it as a method when they're struggling&nbsp;
with sleep. They will actually masturbate to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.340 --> 00:13:22.260
try to help their sleep. So masturbation is not&nbsp;
as impactful as intercourse based on the data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.260 --> 00:13:30.360
but it's a sizable benefit that rests in your&nbsp;
hands, I guess. But it's a nontrivial benefit.&nbsp;

00:13:30.360 --> 00:13:37.380
It's a playful topic of course, and we joke, but I&nbsp;
do find it interesting regarding the biology. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.380 --> 00:13:42.720
we've tried to scratch the surface here and dig&nbsp;
down. Firstly, what we found is that the benefit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.720 --> 00:13:50.340
of sex with orgasm on sleep is hormonally&nbsp;
related. But now, it seems to be that when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.240 --> 00:13:57.780
women engage in intercourse that results in&nbsp;
orgasm, there is an increase in a hormone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.780 --> 00:14:02.820
called oxytocin, which I'm sure many people&nbsp;
have heard of. Now to a lesser degree, that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.820 --> 00:14:08.460
there is also an increase in men. But firstly, we&nbsp;
know that oxytocin is a sleep-inducing molecule&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.460 --> 00:14:12.480
and you can inject this into the brains of&nbsp;
different animals. We don't do animal work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.480 --> 00:14:17.520
we do human work, but you can see that&nbsp;
sleep induction benefit. The benefit in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.520 --> 00:14:22.440
men seems to actually be about a different&nbsp;
chemical called vasopressin. And although&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.440 --> 00:14:27.300
that story is just starting to emerge, it&nbsp;
seems to be those two hormones. Oxytocin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.300 --> 00:14:32.100
more so for females, vasopressin for males.
Tim Ferriss: So vasopressin, that's interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.100 --> 00:14:37.860
to me because vasopressin is, as I understand, an&nbsp;
antidiuretic hormone sometimes given to adolescent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.860 --> 00:14:44.040
bed wetters. Also, while what it's prescribed as&nbsp;
desmopressin, I suppose — this is maybe a topic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.040 --> 00:14:49.080
for another time, but seems to have an impact&nbsp;
on short-term memory, which is quite interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.980 --> 00:14:57.420
to me. But how does vasopressin help sleep? Maybe&nbsp;
it just keeps you asleep longer because you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.420 --> 00:15:01.320
not inclined to get up and go to the bathroom. I&nbsp;
don't know. I mean that's one of the reasons why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.820 --> 00:15:06.240
it's prescribed as desmopressin as well,
Dr. Matthew Walker: It's possible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.240 --> 00:15:11.839
but it seems to have a sleep inducing&nbsp;
mechanism and we don't truly understand why —&nbsp;

00:15:11.839 --> 00:15:12.480
Tim Ferriss: Why?
Dr. Matthew Walker: — but we think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.480 --> 00:15:17.460
that it's perhaps working at two different levels&nbsp;
of the brain. One is directly at the level of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.460 --> 00:15:23.160
the brainstem itself, where you're starting&nbsp;
to shut down the wake promoting regions. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.160 --> 00:15:29.160
think we discussed that in the previous episode.&nbsp;
Things like histamine or adrenaline, serotonin.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.160 --> 00:15:36.900
So one component is working at the level of the&nbsp;
brainstem. The second is working at a much smaller&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.900 --> 00:15:41.640
structure, but more in the middle of the brain&nbsp;
called the hypothalamus, which is actually where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.640 --> 00:15:47.640
we have what's called the sleep wake switch. And&nbsp;
it's literally a flip flop device. And there is&nbsp;&nbsp;

