00:38:43.440 --> 00:38:47.760
got a great treatment for you. It's called sleep&nbsp;
restriction therapy." And you hear the kind of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:47.760 --> 00:38:51.828
record scratch and you say, "Hang on a second, I&nbsp;
just told you I'm not getting enough sleep and you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:51.828 --> 00:38:56.460
are telling me we're going to restrict my sleep."
It's actually a poorly termed phrase. It should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:56.460 --> 00:39:03.120
really be called bedtime restriction therapy.&nbsp;
And it works in the following way that right now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:03.120 --> 00:39:07.080
if you take that example, you are in bed for&nbsp;
eight hours and you're sleeping for four hours,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:07.080 --> 00:39:12.420
you have a miserable sleep efficiency. So you&nbsp;
have a sleep efficiency of 50 percent. Half&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:12.420 --> 00:39:16.500
of the amount of time that you're in bed, you're&nbsp;
awake, half the time you're asleep. We want to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:16.500 --> 00:39:21.540
that around about 85 or greater in terms of your&nbsp;
sleep efficiency. That's what we think is healthy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:22.200 --> 00:39:27.360
And so rather than actually having&nbsp;
you stay in bed for any longer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:27.360 --> 00:39:33.480
we do the opposite and we use the biology of&nbsp;
your brain and something called sleep pressure.&nbsp;

00:39:33.480 --> 00:39:39.300
So from the moment that both you and I woke up,&nbsp;
we've been building up a chemical that we've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:39.300 --> 00:39:43.740
sort of discussed with regarding caffeine,&nbsp;
which was adenosine. And the more adenosine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:43.740 --> 00:39:48.600
builds up the sleepier and sleepier you feel,&nbsp;
and the longer that you are awake, the heavier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:48.600 --> 00:39:52.920
that weight of sleepiness will be weighing you&nbsp;
down. So just take the extreme of let's say you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:52.920 --> 00:39:59.640
pull an all-nighter or you're awake for 36 hours.&nbsp;
Imagine how tired you are at that point when you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:59.640 --> 00:40:04.140
get into bed that following night. It's almost as&nbsp;
though nothing's going to stop you. Earthquakes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:04.140 --> 00:40:08.880
wildfires, you're just going to get into bed&nbsp;
