00:47:18.300 --> 00:47:24.240
of any mental activity upon awakening. If you use&nbsp;
that loose definition, you dream in almost every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:24.240 --> 00:47:30.480
stage of sleep. But what most people think of&nbsp;
when you say dreaming are REM sleep dreams. Those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:30.480 --> 00:47:37.020
are the emotional, the narrative, the bizarre,&nbsp;
the strange. That's really the state from which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:37.020 --> 00:47:44.460
you dream. And when we look at that sleep stage&nbsp;
inside of the MRI scanner, firstly vast swaths&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:44.460 --> 00:47:50.640
of the visual cortex start lighting up in your&nbsp;
brain. The motor regions of the brain erupt in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:50.640 --> 00:47:56.820
activity. Emotional centers of the brain become&nbsp;
active as well as memory centers of the brain.&nbsp;

00:47:56.820 --> 00:48:01.860
And in fact, some parts of your brain are up to&nbsp;
30 percent more active when you're in dream sleep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:01.860 --> 00:48:07.140
than when you're awake, which is stunning to&nbsp;
me because we often think of sleep as just this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:07.140 --> 00:48:13.320
passive unconscious state. But metabolically&nbsp;
from the brain perspective, it's wild.&nbsp;

00:48:14.280 --> 00:48:19.740
But the freakish part is what happens to another&nbsp;
region of your brain called the prefrontal cortex,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:19.740 --> 00:48:24.180
and particularly the left and the right&nbsp;
side, which sits just above your eyes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:24.960 --> 00:48:29.160
And it's particularly a region called the dorsal&nbsp;
lateral prefrontal cortex, left and right side&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:29.160 --> 00:48:34.800
of the frontal lobe. That part of the brain,&nbsp;
normally when it's active, acts like the CEO of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:34.800 --> 00:48:41.760
the brain that makes high level sort of executive&nbsp;
top-down control decisions. And it allows you to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:41.760 --> 00:48:48.060
have not just rational decisions but volitional&nbsp;
decisions. In other words, you are choosing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:48.060 --> 00:48:53.580
make that choice and you're choosing to do that&nbsp;
action. That's part of the prefrontal cortex job.&nbsp;

00:48:54.240 --> 00:48:59.700
That part of the brain goes offline when we go&nbsp;
into REM sleep. Your dorsal lateral prefrontal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:59.700 --> 00:49:05.340
cortex doesn't erupt with activity like many&nbsp;
of these other regions. It is suppressed in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:05.340 --> 00:49:11.580
its activity. And that I think is our current&nbsp;
explanation as to why your dreams are so visual,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:11.580 --> 00:49:20.160
so motoric, filled with past memories, also just&nbsp;
highly emotional. But they're utterly bizarre,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:20.160 --> 00:49:26.340
utterly erratic, and you have no volitional&nbsp;
control. The prison guards have left the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:26.340 --> 00:49:32.760
building and the prisoners are running amuck.&nbsp;
Your prefrontal cortex is down. And so that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:32.760 --> 00:49:37.680
one of the fascinating parts of dream sleep&nbsp;
neurologically — as well as the body by the way.&nbsp;

00:49:38.580 --> 00:49:43.500
I think we probably didn't discuss this&nbsp;
before, but when you go into dream sleep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:43.500 --> 00:49:50.760
your brain paralyzes your body. You are completely&nbsp;
limp. So if I were to come into the bedroom and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:50.760 --> 00:49:56.340
just pick you up during REM sleep, you would&nbsp;
be like a ragdoll. All of your skeletal muscles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:56.340 --> 00:50:02.340
are inhibited. And people don't need to worry,&nbsp;
by the way. Your involuntary skeletal muscles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:02.340 --> 00:50:06.600
that control your respiration and your heart,&nbsp;
they're not affected, but all of your voluntary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:06.600 --> 00:50:11.340
muscles are because your brainstem, which is&nbsp;
where the REM and non-REM mechanisms play out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:12.060 --> 00:50:17.280
Not only does it beam an activating signal up&nbsp;
into the dreaming brain, it sends one down the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:17.280 --> 00:50:21.420
spinal cord and it paralyzes what's called&nbsp;
the alpha motor neurons of the spinal cord&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:21.420 --> 00:50:26.940
that control your voluntary skeletal muscles.
So you are locked into a physical incarceration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:26.940 --> 00:50:33.240
during REM sleep, during dream sleep, which by the&nbsp;
way explains something called REM sleep paralysis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:33.240 --> 00:50:37.740
upon awakening and adequately explains most&nbsp;
alien abductions. But we don't need to go there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:37.740 --> 00:50:44.880
We can always move on to what's different about&nbsp;
dreaming when it comes to lucidity or just pause&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:44.880 --> 00:50:49.560
there and go into anything that comes to mind.
Tim Ferriss: No, let's jump into lucid dreaming.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:49.560 --> 00:50:55.380
I'm ready. I'm done now. And I do want to&nbsp;
talk about tracking of time or perception&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:55.380 --> 00:51:00.000
of time at some point. But I think we&nbsp;
can do that vis-à-vis lucid dreaming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:00.000 --> 00:51:03.120
because I have some yes questions, so I'll&nbsp;
get there. But let's jump into lucid dreaming.&nbsp;

00:51:03.120 --> 00:51:09.300
Dr. Matthew Walker: Yeah, lucid dreaming. It used&nbsp;
to be a complete charlatan science back in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:09.300 --> 00:51:15.900
'60s and the '70s. To suggest that you could gain&nbsp;
control over a normally non-volitional process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:15.900 --> 00:51:23.880
only added insult to the idiotic nature of this&nbsp;
thing called sleep and dreaming by itself. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:23.880 --> 00:51:28.020
now you're telling me, okay, we've got these&nbsp;
things called dreams, and also you claim to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:28.020 --> 00:51:32.940
able to control them and do whatever you like&nbsp;
in them. Really? Show me some good science.&nbsp;

00:51:32.940 --> 00:51:39.600
But now we have some exquisite science and it's&nbsp;
not even debated anymore. It's not a charlatan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:39.600 --> 00:51:46.440
science. And the first experiments actually&nbsp;
come back to our first topic, which was sleep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:46.440 --> 00:51:54.480
and sex. The first experiments used orgasm and&nbsp;
ejaculation as the test. So with a lucid dreamer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:54.480 --> 00:52:00.420
when they are asleep, just like you and I, when&nbsp;
we're not lucid dreaming, they are paralyzed. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:00.420 --> 00:52:06.480
they've got no good way to tell you, "Okay, I'm&nbsp;
now lucid," and they have no way to tell you what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:06.480 --> 00:52:10.860
I'm dreaming about and what's happening,&nbsp;
except that they do, which is their eyes.&nbsp;

00:52:10.860 --> 00:52:16.740
Because one of the two muscle groups that is&nbsp;
spurred from the voluntary skeletal paralysis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:16.740 --> 00:52:22.200
of your muscles are the extraocular muscles that&nbsp;
move your eyes up and down and left. And that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:22.200 --> 00:52:27.060
why by the way, you still have rapid eye movements&nbsp;
during REM sleep, because those muscles are not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:27.060 --> 00:52:32.880
inhibited. So we can now generate essentially a&nbsp;
language between me, the experimenter, and you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:32.880 --> 00:52:41.160
the lucid dreamer, which is like an ocular Morse&nbsp;
code. And you can be telling me, using predefined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:41.820 --> 00:52:46.380
eye movement instructions, what's going on.
So at the moment you become lucid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:46.380 --> 00:52:50.640
you've gone into dream sleep, and I can&nbsp;
see that in the next door sleep control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:50.640 --> 00:52:55.320
center. And then all of a sudden I see, based&nbsp;
on your eye movement traces that I'm measuring,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:55.320 --> 00:53:01.380
you do three deliberate flicks to the left.&nbsp;
And that's your signal to me that "Me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:01.380 --> 00:53:08.340
the participant, I'm now lucid." Okay, great.&nbsp;
Now then you're going to do two big circular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:08.340 --> 00:53:13.800
movements to the right. And that's you saying, "In&nbsp;
my dream, I'm now clenching my right hand and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:13.800 --> 00:53:20.040
moving your eyes circular to the left. That's me&nbsp;
moving my left hand." And so you can be telling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:20.040 --> 00:53:23.880
me every time that you're doing something&nbsp;
in your dream what it is that you're doing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:23.880 --> 00:53:29.340
which is just — I mean, could you imagine?
Tim Ferriss: And then if you're like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:29.340 --> 00:53:33.300
"If you like that trick, now watch me ejaculate!"
Dr. Matthew Walker: Well, that was exactly what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:33.300 --> 00:53:39.000
they did. So then they said, okay, you claim to&nbsp;
be able to — because when you control — lucid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:39.000 --> 00:53:45.600
dreaming, by the way, the definition, is just that&nbsp;
you are aware that you are dreaming as you are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:45.600 --> 00:53:49.800
dreaming. That's our basic scientific definition.&nbsp;
But what most people in the lay public think of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:49.800 --> 00:53:53.400
as lucid dreaming is not just that you know&nbsp;
that you're dreaming, you take control over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:53.400 --> 00:53:57.480
your dreams. And of course, if you can control&nbsp;
your dreams, one of the things that people enjoy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:57.480 --> 00:54:02.100
doing is having sex and having an orgasm.
Tim Ferriss: That's all they're going to do.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:02.100 --> 00:54:04.427
That's all they're going to do.
Dr. Matthew Walker: Yeah, exactly.&nbsp;

00:54:04.427 --> 00:54:06.180
Tim Ferriss: Orgy all — everywhere.
Dr. Matthew Walker: It was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:06.180 --> 00:54:10.480
original dreamscape pornography. Forget&nbsp;
PornHub, you just had to lucid dream.&nbsp;

00:54:10.480 --> 00:54:14.640
Tim Ferriss: It's a heavier lift, but yeah.
Dr. Matthew Walker: It is. But I could argue it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:14.640 --> 00:54:19.860
well worth it. So we've got these experiments now&nbsp;
— and by the way, we've done imaging studies that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:19.860 --> 00:54:25.380
are less messy for the experimenter to — but I&nbsp;
should come explain that by the way. People would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:25.380 --> 00:54:30.000
then say, "Okay, I'm now starting to have sex and&nbsp;
I'm now just about to have an orgasm." And then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:30.000 --> 00:54:34.920
they would say, "I've just had my orgasm." And you&nbsp;
can go into the room and sure enough, with males&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:34.920 --> 00:54:40.080
especially, you've got proof positive data that&nbsp;
what they said was real. And hence, you've just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:40.080 --> 00:54:45.420
scientifically proven lucid dreaming is great.
We've got much more sophisticated MRI scanning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:45.420 --> 00:54:49.440
methods now, and I won't bore you&nbsp;
with the studies, but it's very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:49.440 --> 00:54:57.720
clear that we know that lucid dreaming is real.
The next question then was how do people lucid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:57.720 --> 00:55:04.320
dream? What is the underlying neural mechanism?&nbsp;
And this comes back to the prefrontal cortex&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:04.320 --> 00:55:09.420
and those dorsal lateral prefrontal parts of the&nbsp;
brain, which are the volitional control centers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:09.420 --> 00:55:15.360
which go offline. What we found is that activity,&nbsp;
both the electrical activity and also when you put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:15.360 --> 00:55:21.120
people in scanners, the imaging activity in&nbsp;
those regions, starts to ramp back up again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:21.120 --> 00:55:30.300
as if when you become lucid, you regain your&nbsp;
rational logical control over the dream state. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:30.300 --> 00:55:37.080
so the prefrontal cortex gives you that capacity&nbsp;
to take back what you lost, which is volition.&nbsp;

00:55:38.400 --> 00:55:44.220
There was a study recently, however, that I&nbsp;
think did a pretty good job to say that may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:44.220 --> 00:55:51.480
not be the case, that some of those are results&nbsp;
are confounded by the method of the analysis and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:51.480 --> 00:55:55.920
the method of the recording. So I think it's — the&nbsp;
jury is still a little bit out, but even in that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:55.920 --> 00:56:00.000
study, they demonstrated that there's something&nbsp;
about their brainwaves that is radically different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:00.000 --> 00:56:06.960
at that fulcrum point where they shift from going&nbsp;
from non-lucid dreaming to lucid dreaming. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:06.960 --> 00:56:11.100
not a question of if, it's just how.
Tim Ferriss: What is the jury still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:11.100 --> 00:56:13.800
out on? I'm sorry, I missed that.
Dr. Matthew Walker: So the jury is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:13.800 --> 00:56:19.680
still out on this idea that the electrical&nbsp;
bursting brainwave activity is — it comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:19.680 --> 00:56:24.360
back online over the prefrontal cortex.&nbsp;
And what they were finding is that it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:24.360 --> 00:56:29.580
potentially an artifact of the eye movements&nbsp;
that start to happen. So the eye movements,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:29.580 --> 00:56:33.540
when you're moving those muscles, those&nbsp;
extraocular muscles, you move muscles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:33.540 --> 00:56:40.200
using electrical impulses. And those electrical&nbsp;
impulses that are coming from the eye will also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:40.200 --> 00:56:44.580
bleed up into the brain. And when you&nbsp;
get all of this frenetic eye movement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:44.580 --> 00:56:51.633
it starts to masquerade as if it's the prefrontal&nbsp;
cortex which sits directly above them as getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:51.633 --> 00:56:59.220
reengaged. And hence, when you control for that,&nbsp;
you don't see as powerful an argument for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:59.220 --> 00:57:03.240
re-engagement of the prefrontal cortex.
Tim Ferriss: What is your personal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:03.240 --> 00:57:08.760
experience, if any, with lucid dreaming?
Dr. Matthew Walker: Unfortunately, I am not a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:09.420 --> 00:57:14.580
common lucid dreamer. It's happened probably&nbsp;
about two or three times in my life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:15.240 --> 00:57:23.340
And when it's happened, it's immensely&nbsp;
pleasurable. Have I tried to curate it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:23.340 --> 00:57:28.020
develop it, and use different techniques? And we&nbsp;
can speak about the current techniques that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:28.020 --> 00:57:34.920
out there in the science. I have not chosen to&nbsp;
do that. And it's not necessarily because I see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:34.920 --> 00:57:39.960
in the science anything to be worried about.&nbsp;
I can steel man both sides of that argument.&nbsp;

00:57:39.960 --> 00:57:45.900
We know that only about 10 to 20 percent of&nbsp;
the population are natural lucid dreamers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:46.620 --> 00:57:50.940
And you could argue from an evolutionary&nbsp;
standpoint that if lucid dreaming is so adaptive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:50.940 --> 00:57:56.760
so beneficial to us as Homo sapiens, then the&nbsp;
reverse would be true, that Mother Nature would've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:56.760 --> 00:58:02.340
heavily selected it, and 80 to 90 percent&nbsp;
of the population would be lucid dreamers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:02.340 --> 00:58:08.880
But I can push back on my own argument because&nbsp;
there's a desperate flaw in that logic. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:08.880 --> 00:58:14.700
argument assumes that we've stopped evolving. And&nbsp;
we haven't, right? So maybe that 10 to 20 percent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:14.700 --> 00:58:20.160
of natural lucid dreamers are the forefront of&nbsp;
hominid evolution and we don't need to be worried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:20.160 --> 00:58:27.120
about AI — we need to be worried about the lucid&nbsp;
dreamers becoming the next super race of humanity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:27.120 --> 00:58:34.380
So I wrestle between those two, but if it's that&nbsp;
volume though, that 80 to 90 percent of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:34.380 --> 00:58:41.040
don't naturally do it at this stage after 3.X&nbsp;
million years of evolution of hominid development.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:41.040 --> 00:58:49.740
My sense is that do I think that I understand&nbsp;
better than Mother Nature's blueprint exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:49.740 --> 00:58:54.300
what I should be serving up in my dreams at&nbsp;
night to get all of the benefits of dreams. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:54.300 --> 00:58:58.920
by the way, we know that dreaming, above and&nbsp;
