01:00:01.680 --> 01:00:05.940
That's one argument, but I don't think&nbsp;
that's a particularly good argument to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:05.940 --> 01:00:09.780
say that if you are a lucid dreamer you need&nbsp;
to be worried or if you want to lucid dream&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:09.780 --> 01:00:15.060
you should not engage in some of the different&nbsp;
methods that we've developed to improve it.&nbsp;

01:00:15.060 --> 01:00:19.560
Tim Ferriss: I'm curious what the state of the&nbsp;
art is from a scientific perspective, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:19.560 --> 01:00:28.980
people may hear the term lucid dreaming and think&nbsp;
that it is a relativity new construct/exercise of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:28.980 --> 01:00:33.420
the human mind, but that's not true at all.&nbsp;
I mean, at least for thousands of years in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:34.020 --> 01:00:36.997
Tibetan practices and elsewhere —
Dr. Matthew Walker: Exactly.&nbsp;

01:00:36.997 --> 01:00:42.360
Tim Ferriss: — there have been elaborate&nbsp;
descriptions of training and philosophical&nbsp;&nbsp;

