01:18:54.180 --> 01:19:00.120
brainwave called a sleep spindle, and the more&nbsp;
of those sleep spindles that you are having, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:00.120 --> 01:19:04.980
greater the restoration of your learning ability.&nbsp;
So the way we did the study, we took a group of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:04.980 --> 01:19:10.440
people and we had them try and learn a whole set&nbsp;
of information at midday, and then we brought&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:10.440 --> 01:19:15.000
them back and then at 6:00 pm we had them&nbsp;
learn an additional new set of information.&nbsp;

01:19:15.000 --> 01:19:19.440
And so we could simply compare what's your&nbsp;
learning capacity at time point one versus&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:19.440 --> 01:19:26.880
time point two. But then, half of the subjects&nbsp;
had a nap in the middle of that six-hour period,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:26.880 --> 01:19:32.100
the others did not nap, they remained awake. And&nbsp;
there was a deviation, there was a separation such&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:32.100 --> 01:19:36.840
that those people who napped, they actually had&nbsp;
a restoration of their learning ability. Those&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:36.840 --> 01:19:43.200
people that didn't had a deterioration in their&nbsp;
learning ability. And the greater the number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:43.200 --> 01:19:47.940
spindles during the nap, the sleep spindles&nbsp;
that happen during what we call non-rapid&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:47.940 --> 01:19:53.580
eye movement sleep, the greater the refreshment&nbsp;
and the recuperation of their learning ability.&nbsp;

01:19:53.580 --> 01:19:56.160
Tim Ferriss: How long were the naps?
Dr. Matthew Walker: Yeah, so the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:56.160 --> 01:20:01.080
naps there were 90 minutes in duration.&nbsp;
Now, I wouldn't recommend that for most&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:01.080 --> 01:20:04.140
people and I'll sort of come back to why, but —
Tim Ferriss: Is that an ultradian rhythm&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:04.140 --> 01:20:06.900
or is that a bullshit term?
Dr. Matthew Walker: Yeah. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:06.900 --> 01:20:13.560
you were close with suprachiasmatic, and you're&nbsp;
bang on with the ultradian. So ultradian simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:13.560 --> 01:20:21.000
means it's shorter than a day and circadian, when&nbsp;
we describe it, it means approximately or around&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:21.000 --> 01:20:27.480
a day's length. Ultradian, shorter than a day.&nbsp;
And yes, in human beings, our sleep cycle going&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:27.480 --> 01:20:34.800
from non-REM to REM, which we repeat, is every&nbsp;
90 minutes. Now, that's in us human beings. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:34.800 --> 01:20:41.100
different across different species. Our sleep&nbsp;
cycle is actually quite long relative to others.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:41.100 --> 01:20:47.280
For example, birds may just have a non-REM to REM&nbsp;
cycle of about two minutes, which is very, very&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:47.280 --> 01:20:53.220
short. They flip back and forth. Us, 90 minutes.&nbsp;
And so, usually when we do these nap studies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:53.220 --> 01:21:00.780
we give the brain the opportunity to cycle through&nbsp;
both non-REM and REM, so then we give the sleeping&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:00.780 --> 01:21:05.580
brain the chance to say, "Look, this is the&nbsp;
stage of sleep where you're going to see the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:05.580 --> 01:21:10.860
correlation with the benefit that you measure when&nbsp;
you wake that individual up." So we want to give&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:10.860 --> 01:21:18.540
REM and non-REM an equal opportunity to show their&nbsp;
prowess in transacting a functional benefit for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:18.540 --> 01:21:23.880
the brain. That's why we usually do 90 minutes.
But if you're trying to optimize napping,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:23.880 --> 01:21:28.560
I should probably do a podcast on this&nbsp;
myself, on the optimal version of napping,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:28.560 --> 01:21:35.460
the short and long of it is, nap for about 15&nbsp;
to 20 minutes and no longer, because when you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:35.460 --> 01:21:40.320
nap for longer, what happens is that you start&nbsp;
to go down into the deeper stages of non-REM,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:40.320 --> 01:21:45.120
and then when you wake up after 30 or 40 minutes,&nbsp;
you're coming out of the deeper stage of sleep&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:45.120 --> 01:21:50.400
and you have what's called sleep inertia, which&nbsp;
feels like a sleep hangover, and it's even worse&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:50.400 --> 01:21:57.900
when you wake up than it was before you took the&nbsp;
nap. But if you do 15 or 20 minutes, you don't get&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:57.900 --> 01:22:04.740
that heavy hit of sleep inertia, but you still get&nbsp;
sleep benefits. Once you get down to about nine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:04.740 --> 01:22:10.320
eight, or seven minutes, we really don't see&nbsp;
brain or body benefits. Getting to 15 to 20,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:10.320 --> 01:22:16.140
you get some nice cognitive benefits, but you&nbsp;
lose the downside of the sleep inertia affect.&nbsp;

01:22:16.860 --> 01:22:22.140
But I'm digressing, so that's sort of the sleep&nbsp;
before learning, you also need sleep after&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:22.140 --> 01:22:30.780
learning, however, to then take those new memories&nbsp;
and, as we mentioned before, to sort of fixate&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:30.780 --> 01:22:35.100
them into the neural architecture of the brain. It&nbsp;
is like hitting the save button, but the problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:35.100 --> 01:22:40.680
is, it's not digital in your Word document, which&nbsp;
happens in milliseconds. The saving of memories in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:40.680 --> 01:22:45.900
the brain is an organic process, it requires the&nbsp;
building of new proteins between your synapses, so&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:45.900 --> 01:22:53.220
it takes hours. But it takes hours not just during&nbsp;
wake, but during sleep. And there, what we found&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:53.220 --> 01:23:00.000
is that it's during deep non-REM sleep or deep&nbsp;
non-rapid eye movement sleep that the brain will&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:00.660 --> 01:23:06.600
cement those new memories. It's almost like&nbsp;
that scene in Jurassic Park where the amber&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:06.600 --> 01:23:14.280
has fixated the fly in perpetuity. And sleep&nbsp;
futureproofs the information within your brain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:14.940 --> 01:23:24.120
And this perhaps comes onto both the mechanism,&nbsp;
but also the idea of time that we discussed or&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:24.120 --> 01:23:26.580
that we should discuss, perhaps.
