01:34:46.140 --> 01:34:51.900
of course, that was the last time I was ever asked&nbsp;
to write an op-ed for the Crimson. I suppose that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:51.900 --> 01:34:55.440
told me everything I needed to know. But&nbsp;
it comes on to my point, which is this:&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:55.440 --> 01:35:01.800
what's the goal of education? The goal of&nbsp;
education, in my mind, is twofold. The first is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:01.800 --> 01:35:07.020
to brainwash you into thinking for yourself. The&nbsp;
second is for you to gain long-term information.&nbsp;

01:35:08.520 --> 01:35:13.020
We did a study where we suggested, okay,&nbsp;
you haven't learned any of the information,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:13.020 --> 01:35:19.140
and we tested your hypothesis, and sure enough,&nbsp;
yes, time on task returns some degree of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:19.140 --> 01:35:25.740
greater benefit if you pull the all-nighter.&nbsp;
And they were better in terms of the testing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:25.740 --> 01:35:31.140
relative to if you chose, obviously, not to&nbsp;
engage in the material at all. At that point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:31.140 --> 01:35:34.920
there's no learning whatsoever. Of course,&nbsp;
you're worse. What was interesting, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:35.760 --> 01:35:41.040
is that, in the people who had the same amount of&nbsp;
learning opportunity, but then got a full night&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:41.040 --> 01:35:46.500
of sleep afterwards, relative to the next day,&nbsp;
yes, the people who got sleep were better than&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:46.500 --> 01:35:51.780
those people who were sleep-deprived, but&nbsp;
then we brought them back four weeks later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:52.860 --> 01:35:58.020
and then we tested them a second time, and&nbsp;
the difference between those two groups&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:58.020 --> 01:36:03.240
now was magnified almost tenfold.
In other words, the people who had&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:03.240 --> 01:36:07.680
been sleep-deprived the first night, yes, they&nbsp;
could still test to a degree, they were worse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:07.680 --> 01:36:13.560
but they still got some degree of the answers&nbsp;
right, but the amount of catastrophic forgetting&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:13.560 --> 01:36:18.480
that happened across those following four&nbsp;
weeks relative to the group that imprinted&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:18.480 --> 01:36:23.640
those memories and saved them the first night&nbsp;
was far less. There was a lot less forgetting in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:23.640 --> 01:36:29.880
the group that had — and I put this in context&nbsp;
simply because I don't want to simply educate&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:29.880 --> 01:36:36.240
human minds, which is what I do as a job, only for&nbsp;
you to test well the next day, but then, two years&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:36.240 --> 01:36:40.800
later, you've got none of that information in&nbsp;
your brain. What's the point of higher education&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:40.800 --> 01:36:47.700
then? It should be long term. And so it's a very&nbsp;
insightful suggestion that you make, and we tried&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:47.700 --> 01:36:51.240
to test it. I should publish some of that data.
Tim Ferriss: Question on that study,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:51.240 --> 01:37:00.180
were the groups randomly assigned, or were the&nbsp;
members of each cohort choosing which cohort&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:00.180 --> 01:37:01.500
they wanted to belong to?
Dr. Matthew Walker: No,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:01.500 --> 01:37:04.200
they were randomly assigned.
Tim Ferriss: I was just wondering&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:04.200 --> 01:37:08.280
because I was thinking, if somebody chose to be&nbsp;
in the sleep-deprivation group, their self-care&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:08.280 --> 01:37:12.420
might suck over the subsequent four weeks.
Dr. Matthew Walker: Yeah. Now I would say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:12.420 --> 01:37:15.900
though, the study is partly flawed in&nbsp;
terms of its selection bias because,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:15.900 --> 01:37:20.280
ahead of time, we tell them in the consent&nbsp;
form, "Look, you could be in a group that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:20.280 --> 01:37:25.560
is going to be sleep-deprived or a group that&nbsp;
you're going to have your sleep well-rested&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:26.100 --> 01:37:30.360
and full, and so would you like&nbsp;
to continue?" In some ways,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:30.360 --> 01:37:33.360
they know that they're rolling the dice,&nbsp;
they could or could not be sleep-deprived,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:33.360 --> 01:37:39.660
and so you could argue there's still a selection&nbsp;
bias in that regard, but we don't do selection&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:39.660 --> 01:37:42.780
assignment. We do random assignment.
Tim Ferriss: Science is hard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:42.780 --> 01:37:44.700
man. It's like people, I shouldn't say people,&nbsp;
that's too broad, but a lot of folks don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:44.700 --> 01:37:47.406
realize just how hard it is, right?
Dr. Matthew Walker: It is, but —&nbsp;

01:37:47.406 --> 01:37:48.947
Tim Ferriss: It's really hard.
Dr. Matthew Walker: — it's so rewarding though.&nbsp;

01:37:48.947 --> 01:37:51.780
Tim Ferriss: It is. It is.
Dr. Matthew Walker: At the moment when we analyze&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:51.780 --> 01:37:56.880
the data, and I'm sitting there with a student&nbsp;
or I'm analyzing data, and we finally run the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:56.880 --> 01:38:05.400
statistics, at that moment in time, if I'm lucky,&nbsp;
I know something that has never been understood in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:05.400 --> 01:38:11.880
the entirety of human civilization, and I cannot&nbsp;
tell you how much of a thrill and a privilege that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:11.880 --> 01:38:18.660
is. And so is science hard? It's brutally&nbsp;
hard, but just that alone, the hedonic rush&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:18.660 --> 01:38:25.241
that you get from de novo knowledge, gosh, it's&nbsp;
never left me, and I don't think it ever will.&nbsp;

01:38:25.241 --> 01:38:29.160
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. Yeah, what a drug.
Dr. Matthew Walker: It's quite the drug.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:29.160 --> 01:38:34.080
Get a belt around my arm, the vein is up, and&nbsp;
I will go intravenous with that all day long.&nbsp;

01:38:34.080 --> 01:38:38.340
Tim Ferriss: I want to ask you about sleep&nbsp;
spindles because you mentioned that in the context&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:38.340 --> 01:38:44.700
of napping and how that seems to be a determining&nbsp;
factor or a critical factor in later performance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:44.700 --> 01:38:47.880
cognitive performance. I want to come back&nbsp;
to see if there are any levers you can pull,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:47.880 --> 01:38:52.080
even if you wouldn't recommend them for a long&nbsp;
period of time, right? Are there levers you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:52.080 --> 01:38:56.700
pull to increase the frequency or amplitude of&nbsp;
these sleep spindles? And I say that not really&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:56.700 --> 01:39:01.740
knowing sleep spindles well, but I'm planting&nbsp;
that seed, so I'm going to let that germinate.&nbsp;

01:39:01.740 --> 01:39:07.560
But, first, I want to tell you a personal&nbsp;
experience and see how you might speak to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:07.560 --> 01:39:13.740
it because I have a pretty good out-of-the-box&nbsp;
memory, I think my hardware and genetics are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:13.740 --> 01:39:22.200
reasonably good for it, but I've had a few outlier&nbsp;
experiences that are still, to this day, stand-out&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:22.200 --> 01:39:31.500
experiences, and one of them was, while I was&nbsp;
studying at a university in China, and we were&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:31.500 --> 01:39:39.120
responsible, at the advanced levels, for being&nbsp;
able to produce, so not just recognize, but also&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:40.080 --> 01:39:45.660
actively write many thousands of characters,&nbsp;
and some of these characters have 15,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:45.660 --> 01:39:51.900
20 strokes. They're very intricate.
And I had this experience of getting very sick.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:51.900 --> 01:39:55.920
I can't remember exactly what caused it, maybe I&nbsp;
never learned what caused it, but I had a fever.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:57.000 --> 01:40:03.240
And there was this big exam the next&nbsp;
day, and it was mostly characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:03.240 --> 01:40:07.260
but in the context of essays. It was&nbsp;
not just memorizing characters and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:07.260 --> 01:40:12.840
being familiar with new terminology, some of it,&nbsp;
very abstract. It's not like the cat, it's like&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:14.280 --> 01:40:20.580
hyperinflation, that kind of stuff. And&nbsp;
I was bedridden because I had a fever,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:20.580 --> 01:40:23.700
but I knew this test was coming the&nbsp;
next day, and I had a very high fever.&nbsp;

01:40:24.600 --> 01:40:34.080
And the way that my studying proceeded was I would&nbsp;
wake up for 30 minutes and study super intensely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:34.080 --> 01:40:39.360
and then I would feel really sleepy and I would&nbsp;
just fall asleep right on the book on the bed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:39.360 --> 01:40:45.840
and then I would wake up and I would do another&nbsp;
30-minute burst and then fall asleep. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:45.840 --> 01:40:51.600
when I went into the test the next day, I was&nbsp;
feeling somewhat better, but I'd slept a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:51.600 --> 01:40:56.580
the previous day, and I'd studied a lot of the&nbsp;
previous day and it was intended to be, I want&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:56.580 --> 01:41:05.700
to say, maybe a 90-minute or two-hour exam. And I&nbsp;
finished the exam in something like 25 minutes and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:05.700 --> 01:41:09.360
I handed it in and they were like, "What are you&nbsp;
doing?" and I was like, "I think I'm done," and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:09.360 --> 01:41:15.660
they're like, "You can't be done," and I was done.
Not only was I done, but I scored 95-plus percent&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:16.740 --> 01:41:25.500
on the test, and that blew me away and I don't&nbsp;
really have a great way to explain it. And maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:25.500 --> 01:41:30.720
it was just a freak incident and I'm reading&nbsp;
too far into it, but I've spent a lot of time&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:30.720 --> 01:41:34.320
around very, very, very smart people, including&nbsp;
people who have very impressive memories, and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:34.320 --> 01:41:38.520
for me personally, in my life, I think I've&nbsp;
done a good job of learning many different&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:38.520 --> 01:41:45.600
skills and memorizing many different things, but&nbsp;
that particular test stands out as very unusual.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:46.680 --> 01:41:50.220
Do you have any thoughts on that?
Dr. Matthew Walker: Yeah, I do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:50.220 --> 01:41:57.240
and I think it comes back to the neural mechanisms&nbsp;
of memory consolidation. There are at least two&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:57.240 --> 01:42:03.840
different mechanisms, non-mutually exclusive, that&nbsp;
promote the cementing of that new information,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:03.840 --> 01:42:09.600
but also the freeing up of your short-term&nbsp;
memory capacity. And what we've discovered,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:09.600 --> 01:42:16.980
we and others have done this work, the first&nbsp;
method is a file transfer mechanism that, when you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:16.980 --> 01:42:23.640
go into deep non-REM sleep, which, by the way, is&nbsp;
dialed up by way of infection, when you get sick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:23.640 --> 01:42:29.460
you have an increase in sleep drive, particularly&nbsp;
for deep non-REM sleep. We know why. There are a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:29.460 --> 01:42:35.340
number of immune factors, inflammatory factors,&nbsp;
that call up to play this thing called deep sleep.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:35.340 --> 01:42:40.440
Those immune factors are things like cytokines&nbsp;
and they are very sleep-inducing. That's why&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:40.440 --> 01:42:44.760
you typically want to curl up and get into bed.&nbsp;
That's why you typically sleep more when you are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:44.760 --> 01:42:53.040
sick because the human organism understands that&nbsp;
the very best health insurance policy that it has,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:53.040 --> 01:42:58.320
the very best Swiss Army knife of health,&nbsp;
is this tool called sleep, and it brings&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:58.320 --> 01:43:04.920
all of the armament of sleep to the call.
Part of the reason you were sleeping so much&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:04.920 --> 01:43:10.080
is because you were undergoing the infection.&nbsp;
You had a supercharged amount of deep sleep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:10.080 --> 01:43:16.800
it turns out. What happens, the first mechanism&nbsp;
of deep sleep, is that during — and it's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:16.800 --> 01:43:22.140
just the sleep spindles, and it's not just the&nbsp;
big, powerful, slow waves of deep non-REM sleep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:22.140 --> 01:43:27.540
it's the combination of those two that we've&nbsp;
discovered. We've published some work where,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:27.540 --> 01:43:34.800
as long as you get this perfect timing between a&nbsp;
slow wave and then a sleep spindle, it's almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:34.800 --> 01:43:41.280
like making sure that the drummer is directly on&nbsp;
the beat in this rhythmic capacity, so you have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:41.280 --> 01:43:46.320
slow wave and then you have a sleep spindle riding&nbsp;
on top of it, this electrical burst of activity.&nbsp;

01:43:46.320 --> 01:43:52.440
The slow waves, they go up and down maybe once or&nbsp;
twice a second. The sleep spindles, they burst at&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:52.440 --> 01:43:59.940
about 14 or 15 times per second, 14 or 15 hertz.&nbsp;
And it's the timing, the combined quality of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:59.940 --> 01:44:05.400
these two deep sleep brainwaves, that will shift&nbsp;
memories from a short-term, vulnerable reservoir,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:05.400 --> 01:44:10.500
the hippocampus, to a more permanent long-term&nbsp;
storage site within the brain and protecting&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:10.500 --> 01:44:18.120
them. And that long-term storage site is the&nbsp;
cortex that sits on top of your brain. I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:18.120 --> 01:44:22.620
want to do computer-brain analogies, but I suppose&nbsp;
the idea would be your hippocampus is a little bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:22.620 --> 01:44:27.900
like a USB stick, that it's very good at running&nbsp;
around during the day and grabbing individual&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:27.900 --> 01:44:33.480
files from different locations, but it has a&nbsp;
limited storage capacity. But then, at night,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:33.480 --> 01:44:37.980
you shift those memories from that short-term&nbsp;
reservoir of the hippocampus to the hard drive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:37.980 --> 01:44:42.900
the cortex, which gives you two benefits.
The first is that that which you learned&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:42.900 --> 01:44:48.060
before sleep is now shifted over and&nbsp;
protected and safe in the new location,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:48.060 --> 01:44:54.360
but the second benefit is that, because you've&nbsp;
cleared out the USB stick of the hippocampus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:54.360 --> 01:45:00.120
when you wake up, you've got a refreshed learning&nbsp;
ability and so you can start acquiring new files&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:00.120 --> 01:45:04.980
all over again. It's a symbiotic relationship&nbsp;
that we've discovered. And I think that's one&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:04.980 --> 01:45:11.640
of the explanations for why you were able to just&nbsp;
rip through the material, do it in a short burst.&nbsp;

01:45:11.640 --> 01:45:15.900
By the way, we know that that's optimal for&nbsp;
learning. It's called massed versus spaced&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:15.900 --> 01:45:22.140
practice. If you try to learn in one long bout,&nbsp;
not great. If you chunk it up into different&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:22.140 --> 01:45:26.880
segments, your learning is much better. You&nbsp;
still spend the same two hours of learning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:26.880 --> 01:45:32.760
but, if you spread it across four hours than two&nbsp;
hours straight, much better. But sleep is a part&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:32.760 --> 01:45:37.440
of that. That's the first of the two mechanisms.
Tim Ferriss: That's fascinating. I have a number&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:37.440 --> 01:45:39.083
of follow-up questions. The first is&nbsp;
just on the — there are many different&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:39.083 --> 01:45:43.120
iterations of spaced repetition, but&nbsp;
in these short-burst study sessions is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:43.800 --> 01:45:48.240
how do you explain the benefit of that versus&nbsp;
longer duration? One thing that leaps to mind&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:48.240 --> 01:45:53.460
for me, which may or may not be true based on&nbsp;
your read of the literature, but is that you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:53.460 --> 01:45:59.580
this primacy and recency effect in any given study&nbsp;
session, but you can, in a sense, keep the — what&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:59.580 --> 01:46:01.980
that would mean is the first thing you&nbsp;
study and the last thing you study,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:01.980 --> 01:46:05.580
you're generally going to recall, say in a word&nbsp;
list, better than things in the middle. Okay.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:06.420 --> 01:46:15.780
Now, if that's true in a four-hour session,&nbsp;
you have, let's just say, this almost Golden&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:15.780 --> 01:46:21.120
Gate Bridge, high-tension wire type of dip in&nbsp;
the middle, but, if you have shorter sessions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:21.120 --> 01:46:27.960
the actual drop of that high tension wire is&nbsp;
going to be less, so your average retention&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:27.960 --> 01:46:33.240
over each individual session will be higher.&nbsp;
And maybe that's just me spouting nonsense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:33.240 --> 01:46:37.260
but that's what jumps to mind. Does that mesh?
Dr. Matthew Walker: No, it does mesh very well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:37.260 --> 01:46:42.660
which is that you're getting the primacy and&nbsp;
the recency. It's like this bow shape where&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:42.660 --> 01:46:46.860
the information in the middle of the list, you&nbsp;
don't remember very well, but the stuff at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:46.860 --> 01:46:51.960
front and the back end of it, you do remember&nbsp;
well. Well, if you do only one learning session,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:51.960 --> 01:46:57.540
you only get two stanchions, either end&nbsp;
of the bridge of primacy and recency, but,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:57.540 --> 01:47:03.480
if you split it up into six chunks, then you're&nbsp;
getting six hits of primacy and recency, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:03.480 --> 01:47:08.040
gives you a greater benefit. That's one of them.
The other we think actually relates to attention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:08.700 --> 01:47:15.180
which is that your ability to focus and attend&nbsp;
and have a spotlight of attentional focus, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:15.180 --> 01:47:20.340
need attention to imprint information, at least&nbsp;
consciously, there is non-conscious learning too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:21.000 --> 01:47:27.120
and when you are constantly time on task,&nbsp;
that spotlight of attention starts to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:27.120 --> 01:47:32.520
fade and your ability to focus on what you&nbsp;
should learn decreases and therefore what&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:32.520 --> 01:47:37.800
you learn decreases. It's not really related&nbsp;
to the fact of your memory fatiguing itself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:37.800 --> 01:47:42.960
but I think there's evidence for that too.
One of the things that I've always been&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:42.960 --> 01:47:47.460
fascinated by, and I want to do the study,&nbsp;
but it's one of those bucket-list studies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:47.460 --> 01:47:53.460
I don't think it's quite as simple as that. I, at&nbsp;
least, have found in my own personal experience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:53.460 --> 01:48:00.300
and heard from others, that I could be learning&nbsp;
information about organic chemistry for an hour,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:00.300 --> 01:48:05.400
and I just know that I'm just starting to fade.&nbsp;
My learning is just getting miserable. It's just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:05.400 --> 01:48:11.280
diminishing returns at this point. But then, if I&nbsp;
close the book and I start trying to learn French,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:11.940 --> 01:48:15.960
I actually think my learning capacity&nbsp;
is renewed and refreshed. And so I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:15.960 --> 01:48:20.520
there is something about the context&nbsp;
of the information that the familiarity&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:20.520 --> 01:48:25.607
versus the novelty can also reinstantiate your&nbsp;
learning ability. Does that make some sense?&nbsp;

01:48:25.607 --> 01:48:28.440
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, yeah, totally makes sense.
Dr. Matthew Walker: I think this idea of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:28.440 --> 01:48:33.060
fatigue, we don't really understand it.
Tim Ferriss: No. Yeah. There seem to be these&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:33.060 --> 01:48:35.687
compartmentalized capabilities. It's very —
Dr. Matthew Walker: Isn't that —&nbsp;

01:48:35.687 --> 01:48:41.520
Tim Ferriss: It's wild. It's so wild. I will say&nbsp;
for folks, also, I use the example of word lists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:41.520 --> 01:48:46.920
but having attempted, somewhat successfully&nbsp;
in most cases, to acquire different&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:48.780 --> 01:48:55.500
skills, motor skills, sports, et cetera,&nbsp;
different capabilities, it could even be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:55.500 --> 01:49:00.780
something like poker, which is a combination&nbsp;
of all sorts of things, that the same type&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:02.100 --> 01:49:09.960
of short duration sessions and breaks help. It's&nbsp;
not limited to pure, what we might consider book&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:09.960 --> 01:49:14.100
learning, at least in my experience. Let me ask&nbsp;
two questions and I'll let you pick the first one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:14.100 --> 01:49:18.480
I'd love to hear you comment on both though.
The first is, if you wanted to put your sleep&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:18.480 --> 01:49:24.900
spindles on hyperdrive for a short period&nbsp;
of time, who knows, final exam, right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:24.900 --> 01:49:33.900
I expect the slow wheels of institutionalized&nbsp;
academia will probably not completely reformat the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:33.900 --> 01:49:43.440
end-of-semester testing default that they have had&nbsp;
for so long, so if someone had infinite resources&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:44.100 --> 01:49:48.840
and they wanted to put their sleep spindles on&nbsp;
hyperdrive, how might they do that? The other one&nbsp;&nbsp;

