Tim Ferriss: How cool.&nbsp;

01:53:12.340 --> 01:53:15.660
Dr. Matthew Walker: So we may almost not&nbsp;
need to worry about that. Yeah, it's wild,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:15.660 --> 01:53:17.640
isn't it? When we made that discovery.
Tim Ferriss: What an amazing piece of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:17.640 --> 01:53:21.600
wetware we carry between our ears. What a thing.
Dr. Matthew Walker: Yeah. The other aspect of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:21.600 --> 01:53:25.320
memory that you could optimize,&nbsp;
and people have been doing this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:25.980 --> 01:53:30.780
and I don't think it's mainstream yet, comes back&nbsp;
to the second mechanism of why sleep is good for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:30.780 --> 01:53:35.580
memory. It's not just about shifting memories from&nbsp;
short term to long term. It's about memory replay.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:35.580 --> 01:53:41.100
And this study, or this story, emerged back in the&nbsp;
1990s when a scientist called Bruce McNaughton at&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:41.100 --> 01:53:47.280
the University of Arizona, he was measuring&nbsp;
the memory cell activity in rats as they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:47.280 --> 01:53:53.520
running around a maze and he was trying to learn&nbsp;
how the hippocampus codes a maze and, basically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:53.520 --> 01:53:59.940
how it builds a memory circuit of the maze. And he&nbsp;
was recording individual cells in the hippocampus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:59.940 --> 01:54:06.060
and he wasn't doing this, but you can just&nbsp;
think of this as each cell has a tone to it and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:06.060 --> 01:54:09.840
as the rat would run around the maze, what&nbsp;
he would hear is the signature of learning&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:09.840 --> 01:54:16.020
that would sound a little bit like (singing).
As the rat was running around the maze, he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:16.020 --> 01:54:21.900
listening to the brain imprinting these memories.&nbsp;
The brilliant part of his study was that he didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:21.900 --> 01:54:27.780
stop recording when the session ended. He kept&nbsp;
recording from that memory center as those rats&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:27.780 --> 01:54:32.760
went into sleep and went into deep non-REM sleep.&nbsp;
And, when they went into deep non REM sleep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:33.300 --> 01:54:40.140
he kept listening. And, when you eavesdrop on the&nbsp;
sleeping brain, not only was the memory replayed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:40.140 --> 01:54:47.520
but it wasn't replayed at waking speed. It&nbsp;
was replayed almost 10 times faster. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:47.520 --> 01:54:54.660
what he was hearing, instead of (singing),&nbsp;
he was hearing — as if these rats were just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:54.660 --> 01:55:00.300
replaying the memory at high frequency speed&nbsp;
as if they're etching the memory into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:00.300 --> 01:55:04.800
glass surface of the brain and scoring&nbsp;
the memory trace even more powerfully.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:06.360 --> 01:55:10.260
We know that memory replay is&nbsp;
a critical component of that.&nbsp;

01:55:11.460 --> 01:55:19.380
Then studies — it probably restarted back with&nbsp;
some French scientists in the early 2000s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:19.380 --> 01:55:25.500
and then there was a big paper in Nature by&nbsp;
a German group, and they tried to hijack the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:25.500 --> 01:55:30.540
system of memory replay, and it was brilliant. It&nbsp;
was so clever. And now Ken Paller at Northwestern&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:30.540 --> 01:55:34.980
has been doing some of these studies. Here's&nbsp;
what they did. They had people learn information,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:34.980 --> 01:55:41.760
but they associated that information with&nbsp;
somatosensory stimuli. And what I mean by&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:41.760 --> 01:55:48.840
that is you're learning a list of words and then,&nbsp;
during that list of words, I am infusing a rose&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:48.840 --> 01:55:56.100
perfume up your nose with a mask, and what you're&nbsp;
doing, the brain is bonding the scent, the odor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:56.100 --> 01:56:01.620
of the rose with the learned information, so&nbsp;
now you've got a bonded memory with the odor.&nbsp;

01:56:02.340 --> 01:56:06.600
Then what they would do is they would&nbsp;
let those individuals fall asleep&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:07.260 --> 01:56:11.040
and, during deep non-REM sleep, when we&nbsp;
believe the memories are being replayed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:11.040 --> 01:56:17.100
they started to reperfuse that rose odor up&nbsp;
their nose. And, as a consequence, the next day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:17.100 --> 01:56:23.880
there was about a 40 to 50 percent improvement&nbsp;
in their memory because they had brute-forced&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:23.880 --> 01:56:29.820
the reactivation of the memory. It's almost as&nbsp;
though they decided to take that memory and give&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:29.820 --> 01:56:36.480
it VIP access to the memory replay during non-REM&nbsp;
sleep, and you start to cherry-pick information.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:36.480 --> 01:56:42.000
And then Ken Paller and others, they started to&nbsp;
look at this and they did it even more cleverly.&nbsp;

01:56:42.000 --> 01:56:47.340
I think the way the game over here in America&nbsp;
is actually called Memory, where it's a set of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:47.340 --> 01:56:54.420
cards all out on the table, and each card has its&nbsp;
own pair. You turn one card over and it's a house&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:54.420 --> 01:56:59.340
and you turn another card over, and somewhere in&nbsp;
the rest of the deck there is another house that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:59.340 --> 01:57:04.320
the match. And then there is one teakettle and&nbsp;
then there is one fire engine. And so they would&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:04.320 --> 01:57:10.740
do all of these things. But they did this on the&nbsp;
computer, and each time they were learning this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:11.280 --> 01:57:16.200
associative spatial layout of information.&nbsp;
When they would turn over the teakettle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:16.200 --> 01:57:20.460
they would hear a teakettle sound, and,&nbsp;
when they'd turn over the fire engine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:20.460 --> 01:57:27.420
they would hear a fire engine sound. Once again,&nbsp;
they were bonding a sound, a congruent semantic&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:27.420 --> 01:57:33.060
sound, with that particular information&nbsp;
visually. And then they let them sleep.&nbsp;

01:57:33.060 --> 01:57:38.580
And, as they went into deep non-REM sleep,&nbsp;
they would replay those sounds at what we call&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:38.580 --> 01:57:42.887
a sub-awakening threshold, where it was loud&nbsp;
enough to get into the brain, but not loud —&nbsp;

01:57:42.887 --> 01:57:45.720
Tim Ferriss: Critical detail for people asleep.
Dr. Matthew Walker: Yeah, please, please,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:45.720 --> 01:57:51.720
please don't do that. And what they were able&nbsp;
to do is, essentially, an even more selective&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:51.720 --> 01:57:58.740
process of cherry-picking. Now, whatever I want to&nbsp;
serve up to the menu of this thing called sleep at&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:58.740 --> 01:58:04.140
night in terms of the different dishes of memory,&nbsp;
I get to choose. I can say, "No, no, no, I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:04.140 --> 01:58:09.420
want that information. I want this particular&nbsp;
information tonight and I'm going to develop&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:09.420 --> 01:58:15.840
this auditory playlist." And so the question then&nbsp;
becomes can you start using that method? Is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:15.840 --> 01:58:20.760
cheap version of that that I'm going to study&nbsp;
organic chemistry and I'm going to blaze up some&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:20.760 --> 01:58:26.760
green tea incense, which I personally really&nbsp;
like, and then, before I go to bed at night,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:26.760 --> 01:58:35.160
am I going to put back on the green tea incense&nbsp;
and start trying to stuff that back up my nose&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:35.160 --> 01:58:42.660
and either improve my memory or burn my house&nbsp;
down and be a scientist no more and chargrill&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:42.660 --> 01:58:46.860
the rest of my body, because it's a desperate&nbsp;
fire safety hazard and you'd never want to do it?&nbsp;

01:58:46.860 --> 01:58:52.200
But this is the idea, that there's something&nbsp;
that we're starting to understand about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:52.200 --> 01:58:57.240
sleeping brain that not just is understanding,&nbsp;
but it gives us the ability to take control. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:57.240 --> 01:59:04.920
at that point, that becomes scary because should&nbsp;
we be taking control of a process that maybe is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:04.920 --> 01:59:10.380
not really our purview to be in control of?
Tim Ferriss: Sounds like the story of&nbsp;&nbsp;

