and then the next level it's five years,&nbsp;
and the next level it's 50 years. And you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:28.200 --> 02:02:34.920
constantly doing this sort of time dilation, as&nbsp;
it were. It comes up with some fascinating ideas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:34.920 --> 02:02:39.060
but science is just getting to that&nbsp;
position now, where we can understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:39.060 --> 02:02:44.553
time during sleep. Because it is strange.&nbsp;
It's a very strange thing, time, with sleep.&nbsp;

02:02:44.553 --> 02:02:50.640
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. I mean, if you sit with that&nbsp;
for a minute, I mean that is one of the strangest&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:51.600 --> 02:02:58.800
experiences that is very hard to fit into our sort&nbsp;
of paradigms of thinking about things right now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:58.800 --> 02:03:05.220
right? I mean, the idea that you could have more&nbsp;
than five minutes, you could have 40 minutes of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:05.880 --> 02:03:11.880
experience, subjective experience, in five&nbsp;
minutes of waking, Earth time. No matter if we&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:11.880 --> 02:03:19.320
take the fidelity of the firsthand experience to&nbsp;
be high, in so much as it being a real experience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

