it raises a lot of very, very&nbsp;
hard-to-answer questions.&nbsp;

02:03:25.968 --> 02:03:32.460
Dr. Matthew Walker: Yeah, it's fascinating too.&nbsp;
And right now, I do have a neural hypothesis for&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:32.460 --> 02:03:37.260
this, and there's a part of the brain called the&nbsp;
insular cortex, that sort of is in the left and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:37.260 --> 02:03:42.180
the right side, but it's buried deep inside. And&nbsp;
it serves many different functions, but one of its&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:42.180 --> 02:03:49.320
functions is the mapping of your bodily state,&nbsp;
of your emotions, and the updating it, from one&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:49.320 --> 02:03:55.260
moment to the next, to the next. So the insular&nbsp;
cortex is consistently mapping the physiological&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:55.260 --> 02:04:00.480
state of your body, from one moment, to the&nbsp;
next, to the next. The reason I bring that up is&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:00.480 --> 02:04:07.620
because the physiological emotional state of your&nbsp;
body contributes to time. How does it do that?&nbsp;

02:04:07.620 --> 02:04:11.520
I think you and I, and everyone listening,&nbsp;
has also had that experience, where they said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

